  "2026-10-10T08:15:29.956Z",codex-vcs-auth-investigator,"4236960214 survives as a local API authorization defect (high confidence), not a proven network escalation: composition-commands.ts:46-57 supplies only NEW tokens; Repository.setAuthority (engine/repo.ts:1000-1003) holds shared writer lease but calls configureAuthority (engine/composition.ts:190-195), which never reads or authorizes the existing grant and atomically replaces authority.json. Disposable real SDK harness: wrong-token obliteration first refused; vcs authority replaced valid v2 without current token; vcs obliterate with new token physically removed the selected file and recorded denial. eraseFile authorizes at engine/erasure.ts:75 and deletes at 183. HTTP dispatcher (engine/serve.ts:797 onward) exposes transport endpoints, no authority/obliterate command. Filesystem operators can already replace grants/delete bytes; this protects restricted command/API callers only. Linked-instance setAuthority replaced the HUB grant, affecting all instances, with no instance-local grant created."
  "2026-10-10T08:15:30.562Z",codex-vcs-auth-investigator,"Authority compatibility/races: actual absence supports initial 3-argument setup. Disposable matrix: version 1, unversioned legacy, unknown version, JSON null and corrupt JSON all reject authorization but setAuthority overwrites each; corrupt JSON reports bad_authority. Historical 37bfae7 establishes unversioned salted SHA-256 legacy schema; explicit v1 support must be justified rather than inferred. Current tests at test/composition.test.ts:944 cover authorization refusals and unversioned regeneration, not denial of valid-v2 rotation; fixture and CLI harness at 298 already reconfigure grants and need current credential after hardening. Repository setAuthority and obliterate serialize on shared objects.lock; authorization is inside the outer repository lease. Lower-level configureAuthority demonstrably writes during a live lease; exported eraseFile authorizes before its own lease, so standalone use can race rotation. Atomic rename/fsync alone does not close check/write races. Direct filesystem concurrency is excluded by ARCHITECTURE.md:378-380."
